candidates.description:
In AttachmentServlet.doGet, after the permission check and 304 handling, the servlet writes a Content-Disposition header built by string-concatenating att.getFileName() directly into the header value:

    res.addHeader( "Content-Disposition", "inline; filename=\"" + att.getFileName() + "\";" );

att.getFileName() is derived from the stored attachment name rather than from a hard-coded value (compare with the 304 check at line 253, which also uses att.getName()). If an attachment name can contain CR/LF or quote characters, the value reaches the header sink unencoded. Servlet containers vary in whether they reject CR/LF inside setHeader/addHeader, so this is a candidate, not a confirmed vulnerability. The same doGet method does show that the author was aware of the sensitivity of user-supplied values in this flow: the 'nextpage' parameter is routed through a validateNextPage(...) helper before sendRedirect (line 304), while the filename is not routed through any visible encoding/sanitization step. The upload-side path that would normally sanitize the attachment name (AttachmentManager / BasicAttachmentProvider) was not within the readable spans, so whether a name containing CR/LF can actually be persisted and later read back here is unverified.

AttachmentServlet.doGet reads the request parameter "nextpage" (line 204) and, after successfully streaming the attachment, issues a redirect to it:

    if( nextPage != null ) {
        res.sendRedirect( validateNextPage( nextPage, m_engine.getURL( WikiContext.ERROR, "", null, false ) ) );
    }

So the user-controlled parameter is passed as the redirect target. The presence of a validateNextPage helper suggests the author intended to constrain the destination (the second argument, the engine's ERROR URL, looks like the intended fallback), but the body of validateNextPage was not within the readable spans, so it is not established whether the check rejects absolute/external URLs (e.g. "//evil.example", "https://evil.example") or protocol tricks. This is a candidate open-redirect / redirect-to-arbitrary-URL weakness contingent on the helper's actual logic; the code as read simply forwards the parameter value into sendRedirect.

In WikiJSPFilter.doFilter the raw request URI is concatenated into logging and watchdog state without any visible encoding:

    NDC.push( m_engine.getApplicationName()+":"+((HttpServletRequest)request).getRequestURI() );
    w.enterState("Filtering for URL "+((HttpServletRequest)request).getRequestURI(), 90 );

The value is the request URI (attacker-controllable path), and it flows into NDC (log4j nested diagnostic context) and the watchdog state string. This is only a candidate for log-injection/response-encoding issues: whether it is exploitable depends on the logging configuration and on whether the watchdog state is rendered back to a user, neither of which was read. No evidence of HTML output of this value was found in the readable span.
